+     *
+     * All this does is set QT_WAYLAND_SHELL_INTEGRATION=layer-shell, and it is
+     * tempting to export that from the session script instead -- but that would
+     * be wrong: LayerShellQt's integration makes EVERY window of the process a
+     * layer surface (createShellSurface() has no fallback to xdg-shell), so any
+     * other Qt program in the session would break.  lxqt-session did, which is
+     * how this was found.  Only the panel wants layer-shell, so only the panel
+     * asks for it.
+     */
+    LayerShellQt::Shell::useLayerShell();
+#endif
+
     LXQtPanelApplication app(argc, argv);
 
+#ifdef LXQT_PANEL_STATIC_WM_BACKEND
+    /*
+     * useLayerShell() above put QT_WAYLAND_SHELL_INTEGRATION=layer-shell into
+     * this process's environment so that Qt would choose the layer-shell
+     * integration when the panel created its first window while building `app`
+     * just above.  Qt reads that variable lazily -- once, on the first
+     * shell-surface creation -- and then caches the choice, which has now
+     * happened, so the variable has served its purpose for the panel.
+     *
+     * If it is left set, though, every program the panel launches from its menus
+     * inherits it and also comes up as a fullscreen, undecorated layer surface
+     * (a terminal with no title bar, a menu that fills the screen).  Clear it now
+     * that Qt has consumed it: the panel keeps layer-shell for its own windows,
+     * and the apps it spawns fall back to the default xdg-shell and are decorated.
+     */
+    qunsetenv("QT_WAYLAND_SHELL_INTEGRATION");
+#endif
+
     return app.exec();
 }
